# The altitude frame a new waypoint gets comes from the screen's selector, as CMB_altmode does.
#
# The claim: choosing terrain and then clicking the map produces a waypoint in frame 10, not 3.
# "50 m" means three different heights depending on this and one of them flies into a hill. And
# the first click is waypoint 1, written after home: home is never one of the clicks, so its
# frame (GLOBAL, 0) is never the frame a click was given.

screen plan
window 1600x1200
budget 5

expect mission.items 0
expect plan.frame relative

# A waypoint in the default frame.
# The map first: a press before its first paint records nothing.
expect map.ready true
click map@0.45x0.40
expect mission.items 1
expect mission.frames ~ relative
expect mission.written ~ ,1:3:16

# Switch to terrain and add two more.
click plan-frame-terrain
expect plan.frame terrain
click map@0.55x0.45
click map@0.60x0.55
expect mission.items 3
expect mission.frames ~ terrain
expect mission.written ~ ,1:3:16,2:10:16,3:10:16
